course: biology
lesson: 43
title: The platypus and the echidna: mammals that lay eggs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mammals are animals with fur or hair that feed their babies milk, and almost all of them give birth to live babies, but five strange Australian and New Guinean animals lay eggs instead.
explain: These egg-laying mammals are called monotremes: the platypus, with a duck-like bill, and four kinds of spiny echidna; they kept an ancient way of having babies that the other mammals lost long ago.
explain: When the first platypus skin reached Britain around 1799, a scientist looked for stitches, sure that a joker had sewn a duck's bill onto a furry animal, like a toy made from bits of two other toys.
explain: In cartoons the platypus is a funny duck-beaver that quacks; the real one never quacks, hunts with its eyes shut by feeling electricity, and the males carry venom.
fact: Mammals have fur or hair and feed their babies milk; it is a myth that all mammals give birth to live young, because monotremes lay eggs.
fact: Only 5 kinds of monotremes live today: the platypus, the short-beaked echidna of Australia and New Guinea, and three long-beaked echidnas found only in New Guinea.
fact: A platypus dives with its eyes, ears and nostrils closed and finds shrimps, worms and insect larvae with tens of thousands of sensors in its soft bill that feel movement and faint electricity.
fact: A mother platypus lays usually 1 to 3 small leathery eggs, often 2, and curls round them in a riverbank burrow for about 10 days; she has no nipples, and the babies lap milk from patches of skin.
fact: A male platypus has a hollow spur on each back ankle that injects venom, very painful to people and able to kill a dog; males use it mostly against other males in the breeding season; never handle a wild platypus.
fact: In 2020 scientists found that platypus fur glows blue-green under ultraviolet light; nobody yet knows whether the glow is useful to the animal.
fact: Around 1799 the naturalist George Shaw checked the first platypus skin for stitches, and egg-laying was finally proved only in 1884.
fact: A platypus stuffs food from the riverbed into its cheek pouches and chews it at the surface with hard horny pads, because adults have no teeth; it must come up to breathe, so filling the pouches saves trips.
fact: An echidna is covered in spines that are thick hairs; it walks slowly and cannot run from danger, so when frightened it curls into a ball or digs straight down until only its spines show.
fact: An echidna has no teeth and catches ants and termites with a long sticky tongue after breaking into their nests with strong claws.
fact: A mother echidna lays one soft egg straight into a pouch; after about 10 days a baby the size of a jellybean, called a puggle, hatches and drinks milk there; when its own spines start to grow, the mother moves it out to a burrow.
fact: Echidnas can survive bushfires by hiding in burrows or logs and lowering their body temperature and heartbeat, a state called torpor; their normal body temperature is only about 32 degrees, and because keeping warm burns food, a cooler body needs less food.
fact: Other mammals give birth: a hedgehog looks a bit like an echidna but has live babies, and a kangaroo gives birth to a tiny live baby that grows in its pouch.
fact: Attenborough's long-beaked echidna was filmed alive for the first time in 2023, after decades with no proof that it still existed.
exercise: What makes an animal a mammal? | It has fur or hair and feeds its babies milk | It always gives birth to live babies, never eggs | It always lives on land and breathes air | It has a backbone, warm blood and lays no eggs ever
exercise: What are mammals that lay eggs called? | Monotremes | Egg-laying marsupials | Furry reptiles | Land amphibians
exercise: How many kinds of egg-laying mammals are alive today? | 5 | 1 | 2 | 50
exercise: How does a platypus find food at the bottom of a creek? | Its bill feels movement and the faint electricity of its prey | It sees the prey clearly with its big wide-open eyes under water | It hears the prey swimming | It smells the prey through its open nostrils
exercise: Where does a mother platypus keep her eggs? | Curled round them in a burrow by the river | In a warm pouch on her belly, like a kangaroo | Floating in a nest on the water | Buried in hot sand on the bank like a turtle's
exercise: How do baby platypuses drink milk? | They lap it from patches of skin on the mother's belly | They suck it from nipples, just like puppies and kittens do | They drink it from the eggshell | They do not drink milk at all
exercise: What is the sharp spur on a male platypus's back leg? | A venom spur, used mostly against other males | A strong claw for digging long burrows in the bank | A spike for catching fish | A leftover toe with no use
exercise: What did scientists see when they shone ultraviolet light on platypus fur? | It glowed blue-green | It turned a bright red | It went completely see-through | It gave off a lot of heat
exercise: Why did a scientist look for stitches on the first stuffed platypus? | He suspected a joke: a duck's bill sewn onto a furry animal | He thought it had been badly hurt in an accident and sewn up | He wanted to know how to make a new one | He was checking whether it was a toy made for children to play with
exercise: How does an adult platypus chew its food? | With hard horny pads, after carrying food up in its cheek pouches | With rows of sharp teeth at the front and back of its flat bill | It swallows everything whole without chewing | With its webbed front feet
exercise: What are an echidna's spines? | Thick hairs | Tiny sharp bones | Feathers that grew hard | Poisonous stings
exercise: What does an echidna do when a dingo comes near? | Curls up or digs down so only its spines show | Runs away much faster than the dingo can follow | Fights back with its sharp teeth | Climbs the nearest tall gum tree to safety
exercise: How does an echidna eat termites? | It breaks into the mound with its claws and licks them up with its sticky tongue | It bites them with rows of tiny teeth | It sucks them up through its long snout just like a straw, a few at a time | It waits for them to crawl into its mouth while it sleeps
exercise: What is a baby echidna called, and where does it live at first? | A puggle, in its mother's pouch | A joey, in a soft nest of dry grass | A cub, in a deep burrow by the river | A chick, under its mother's wing
exercise: How can an echidna survive a bushfire? | It hides in a burrow or log and lowers its temperature and heartbeat | It runs faster than the flames | Its spines cannot burn, so it just walks straight through the fire | It swims across the nearest river every time it smells smoke
exercise: What happened in 2023 to Attenborough's long-beaked echidna? | It was filmed alive for the first time | It was officially declared extinct by scientists | It was found living wild in the forests of Europe | It was seen laying its eggs under water
test: «all mammals have live babies, that's the whole point of mammals» What is right? | No, the platypus and echidnas are mammals that lay eggs | Yes, any animal that lays eggs is a bird or a reptile, simple | Yes, the platypus is a kind of duck | No, all mammals lay eggs
test: In a cartoon, a platypus quacks and hunts with big open eyes. What would a real platypus do? | Dive with its eyes shut and feel for food with its bill | Quack loudly under the water to scare the fish out of the weeds | Hunt only on land by sight | Fly low over the water like a duck
test: «found a platypus on the creek bank, gonna pick it up for a pic» What is the best advice? | Leave it: a male can stab with a venomous spur | Fine, platypuses are totally harmless little things | Fine, just hold its bill shut | Only pick up the ones that glow
test: A poster shows five animals, one platypus and four echidnas. What do all five share? | They are the only egg-laying mammals alive today | They are all marsupials that carry babies in pouches | They all live in South America | They all have sharp teeth
test: «a duck bill + beaver tail?? that thing's fake mate» Why should a scientist be careful before deciding? | Strange things can be real, so check the evidence before deciding | Anything that looks that strange is always fake, so throw it straight out | Scientists never doubt anything | Animals with bills are always birds
test: Why does a platypus fill its cheek pouches before coming up? | It breathes only at the surface, so it saves trips by eating many mouthfuls at once | It is hiding food from the other platypuses that share its stretch of the creek | It cannot swallow under water at all | It wants to feed its eggs on the surface
test: «echidnas = hedgehogs, same thing» What is right? | No, an echidna lays eggs; a hedgehog gives birth to live young | Yes, they are the same animal, just with different names in Australia | Yes, both lay eggs | No, an echidna is a kind of porcupine fish
test: What would happen if a mother echidna kept her puggle in the pouch after its spines grew? | The spines would prick her, so she moves it to a burrow | Nothing at all, because baby spines stay soft forever | The puggle would lay its own egg | The pouch would grow spines too
test: In a game, a platypus hatches from an egg and its mother feeds it worms. What is wrong? | Platypus babies drink their mother's milk | Platypuses do not hatch from eggs | Platypus mothers feed their babies fish bones | Nothing is wrong
test: Why does a cooler echidna body need less food? | Keeping warm burns food, so a cooler body burns less | Cold bodies can make their own food out of air and sunlight | Food freezes inside a cool body | Cool animals never move at all
test: A kangaroo and a platypus are both mammals. How are their babies different? | A kangaroo gives birth to a tiny live baby, a platypus lays eggs | Both lay eggs in a pouch | Both give birth to big live babies | A kangaroo lays eggs in a pouch, and a platypus has live babies in a burrow
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the monotremes? | They are mammals bearing fur and nourishing their young with milk, yet laying eggs | They are reptiles which have acquired fur and the capacity to produce milk | They are birds which have lost the capacity for flight | They are mammals which give birth to young of exceptional size and strength
exam: Identify the principal means whereby the platypus locates its prey beneath the water. | Sensors within the bill which detect movement and faint electrical activity | Acute vision, the eyes remaining open throughout the dive | Hearing, the ears remaining open throughout the entire course of the dive | Echolocation in the manner of the bat
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the spines of the echidna. | The spines are modified hairs, protecting an animal unable to flee rapidly | The spines are hollow and inject a venom sufficient to disable a dingo | The spines are shed and regrown on a daily basis | The spines are feathers of an unusual form, inherited from the ancestral birds
exam: Ascertain the number of species of egg-laying mammals presently extant. | Five | A single species | Precisely two | Twelve
